# Batch 1 source. Q = (verbatim original, translation, work, citation, verify_file)
C=[]
def card(id,unit,era,term,translit,say,gloss,meaning,person,who,lm,q=None,lang="de"):
    C.append(dict(id=id,unit=unit,era=era,term=term,translit=translit,respelling=say,gloss=gloss,meaning=meaning,person=person,who=who,lm=lm,q=q,lang=lang))
SEP="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/";WP="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/";IEP="https://iep.utm.edu/"
# I · Greek beginnings (Presocratics, Socrates, Plato)
card("t-logos",1,"greek","λόγος","lógos","LOH-goss","word, account, reason","For Heraclitus the logos is the rational order that runs through all change, which most people fail to notice. Later Greek thinkers used the same word for argument, definition and the reasoning faculty itself. The Stoics made it the divine reason pervading the cosmos, and John's Gospel opens with it.","Heraclitus","Heraclitus",("Heraclitus",SEP+"heraclitus/"),lang="grc")
card("t-arche",1,"greek","ἀρχή","archḗ","ar-KHAY","beginning, first principle, rule","The first Greek philosophers asked what the archē of everything is: the source from which things come and into which they return. Thales said water, Anaximenes air, Anaximander 'the boundless'. The same word also means 'rule' or 'command', so a first principle is also what governs.","Thales","Thales",("Arche",WP+"Arche"),lang="grc")
card("t-apeiron",1,"greek","ἄπειρον","ápeiron","AH-pay-ron","the boundless, the unlimited","Anaximander's candidate for the first principle: not any one element but something without limits or definite character. Opposites like hot and cold separate out of it and pay each other 'penalty' for their injustice. It is the first abstract concept in Western philosophy.","Anaximander","Anaximander",("Anaximander",IEP+"anaximander/"),lang="grc")
card("t-physis",1,"greek","φύσις","phýsis","FOO-sis","nature, growth","Physis is the way a thing grows and behaves of its own accord, as opposed to what is made by craft or fixed by custom (nomos). The early thinkers were called physikoi because they studied it. The contrast between physis and nomos drove fifth-century debates about whether justice is natural or conventional.","Presocratics","Heraclitus",("Physis",WP+"Physis"),lang="grc")
card("t-nomos",1,"greek","νόμος","nómos","NOH-moss","law, custom, convention","Nomos covers both written law and unwritten custom. The Sophists set it against physis: some argued that conventional justice restrains the strong against nature, others that law is what makes human life possible. Plato's dialogues return to the question again and again.","The Sophists","Protagoras",("Physis and nomos",WP+"Nomos_(sociology)"),lang="grc")
card("t-arete",1,"greek","ἀρετή","aretḗ","ah-reh-TAY","excellence, virtue","Aretē is the excellence of anything at doing what it does: a knife's sharpness, a horse's speed, a person's goodness. Socrates asked whether human aretē could be taught, and Plato and Aristotle identified it with the virtues of character and intellect. 'Virtue' is the usual translation, but 'excellence' is closer.","Plato","Plato",("Arete",WP+"Arete"),("ΜΕΝ. καὶ ἄλλαι πάμπολλαι ἀρεταί εἰσιν, ὥστε οὐκ ἀπορία εἰπεῖν ἀρετῆς πέρι ὅτι ἐστίν·","MENO: And there are a great many other virtues, so it is no trouble to say what virtue is.","Meno","72a","meno.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-aporia",1,"greek","ἀπορία","aporía","ah-por-EE-ah","no way through, impasse","Aporia is the puzzled state in which an argument leaves you stuck. Socrates' early dialogues often end there: the confident speaker discovers he cannot define courage or piety after all. Aristotle made working through aporiai the standard way to begin an inquiry.","Socrates","Socrates",("Aporia",WP+"Aporia"),lang="grc")
card("t-elenchus",1,"greek","ἔλεγχος","élenchos","EH-leng-khoss","testing, refutation","The elenchus is Socrates' method: get someone to state what they believe, then draw out further admissions until they contradict the original claim. It shows the speaker does not know what he thought he knew. Socrates claimed his only wisdom was knowing that he did not know.","Socrates","Socrates",("Socratic method",WP+"Socratic_method"),("ὁ δὲ ἀνεξέταστος βίος οὐ βιωτὸς ἀνθρώπῳ","The unexamined life is not worth living for a human being.","Apology","38a","apol.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-eidos",1,"greek","εἶδος","eîdos","AY-doss","form, look, kind","Plato's Forms (eidē, also ideai) are the unchanging realities that particular things imitate: many beautiful things, one Beauty itself. Only the Forms can be truly known, while the changing world of the senses gives mere opinion. Aristotle kept the word but placed form inside things, as their structure.","Plato","Plato",("Theory of Forms",WP+"Theory_of_forms"),lang="grc")
card("t-anamnesis",1,"greek","ἀνάμνησις","anámnēsis","ah-NAM-nay-sis","recollection","In the Meno and Phaedo, Plato argues that learning is really remembering what the soul knew before birth. Socrates leads an untaught slave boy to a geometric truth only by asking questions. The theory is the ancestor of later doctrines of innate ideas.","Plato","Plato",("Anamnesis",WP+"Anamnesis_(philosophy)"),lang="grc")
card("t-thaumazein",1,"greek","θαυμάζειν","thaumázein","thow-MAH-zane","to wonder, to marvel","Plato and Aristotle both say philosophy begins in wonder: puzzlement at something we do not understand. Socrates tells young Theaetetus that his dizziness at a paradox is a sign of a philosophical nature.","Plato","Plato",("Theaetetus",SEP+"plato-theaetetus/"),("μάλα γὰρ φιλοσόφου τοῦτο τὸ πάθος, τὸ θαυμάζειν· οὐ γὰρ ἄλλη ἀρχὴ φιλοσοφίας ἢ αὕτη","For this feeling, wonder, belongs very much to the philosopher: there is no other beginning of philosophy than this.","Theaetetus","155d","theaet.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-eros",1,"greek","ἔρως","érōs","EH-rose","love, desire","In Plato's Symposium, Diotima teaches Socrates that erōs is longing for what we lack, above all for the beautiful. Rightly guided, it climbs a ladder from one beautiful body to beautiful souls, laws and knowledge, and finally to Beauty itself. Here 'Platonic love' has its origin.","Plato","Plato",("Plato on love",WP+"Symposium_(Plato)"),lang="grc")
# II · Aristotle & Hellenistic
card("t-eudaimonia",2,"greek","εὐδαιμονία","eudaimonía","yoo-dye-moh-NEE-ah","good spirit; flourishing","Aristotle argues that the highest human good is eudaimonia, a whole life lived well, not a passing feeling of happiness. It consists in activity of the soul in accordance with virtue, above all reason. 'Flourishing' captures it better than 'happiness'.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Aristotle's ethics",SEP+"aristotle-ethics/"),("τὸ ἀνθρώπινον ἀγαθὸν ψυχῆς ἐνέργεια γίνεται κατ' ἀρετήν","The human good turns out to be activity of the soul in accordance with virtue.","Nicomachean Ethics","1.7, 1098a16","ne.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-telos",2,"greek","τέλος","télos","TEH-loss","end, goal, completion","For Aristotle, to understand a thing you must know its telos, what it is for. An acorn's telos is the oak, an eye's is seeing. Explanation by ends (teleology) dominated natural philosophy until the scientific revolution, and it survives in ethics.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Teleology",WP+"Teleology"),("ἡ δὲ φύσις τέλος ἐστίν","And nature is an end.","Politics","1.2, 1252b32","pol.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-episteme",2,"greek","ἐπιστήμη","epistḗmē","eh-pis-TAY-may","knowledge, science","Epistēmē is knowledge in the strict sense: understanding why something must be so, demonstrated from first principles. Plato contrasted it with doxa, mere opinion. Aristotle made it one of the intellectual virtues, alongside technē and phronēsis. It gives us 'epistemology'.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Episteme",WP+"Episteme"),lang="grc")
card("t-techne",2,"greek","τέχνη","téchnē","TEKH-nay","craft, art, skill","Technē is skilled know-how that produces something: medicine, shipbuilding, rhetoric. Socrates used it as a model of expert knowledge that can give an account of itself. Aristotle separated it from epistēmē (knowing what cannot be otherwise) and phronēsis (acting well).","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Episteme and techne",SEP+"episteme-techne/"),lang="grc")
card("t-phronesis",2,"greek","φρόνησις","phrónēsis","FROH-nay-sis","practical wisdom, prudence","Phronēsis is the ability to see what to do in particular situations, here and now. For Aristotle no one can be fully virtuous without it, and it cannot be learned from rules alone: it needs experience. It came back into favour in 20th-century virtue ethics.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Phronesis",WP+"Phronesis"),lang="grc")
card("t-energeia",2,"greek","ἐνέργεια","enérgeia","en-ER-gay-ah","activity, actuality","Aristotle coined energeia for actuality, as opposed to dynamis, potentiality. A sleeping geometer has knowledge potentially; when she proves a theorem she has it actually. The pair let him explain change without Parmenides' paradoxes. Our word 'energy' descends from it.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Potentiality and actuality",WP+"Potentiality_and_actuality"),lang="grc")
card("t-ousia",2,"greek","οὐσία","ousía","oo-SEE-ah","being, substance, essence","Ousia, from the verb 'to be', is Aristotle's word for what is most fundamentally real. In the Categories it is the individual thing, this horse or this man; in the Metaphysics the inquiry shifts to form. Latin rendered it substantia and essentia, and Christian theology used it for the one nature of the Trinity.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Aristotle's metaphysics",SEP+"aristotle-metaphysics/"),lang="grc")
card("t-ataraxia",2,"greek","ἀταραξία","ataraxía","ah-tah-rak-SEE-ah","untroubledness, tranquillity","Ataraxia is freedom from mental disturbance, the goal shared by Epicureans and Sceptics. Epicurus sought it by removing the fear of death and of gods; the Sceptics by suspending judgement (epochē), after which tranquillity follows 'like a shadow'.","Diogenes Laërtius","Epicurus",("Ataraxia",WP+"Ataraxia"),("τέλος δὲ οἱ σκεπτικοί φασι τὴν ἐποχήν, ᾗ σκιᾶς τρόπον ἐπακολουθεῖ ἡ ἀταραξία","The Sceptics say the goal is suspension of judgement, which tranquillity follows like a shadow.","Lives of the Philosophers","9.107","dl.xml"),lang="grc")
card("t-apatheia",2,"greek","ἀπάθεια","apátheia","ah-PAH-thay-ah","freedom from passion","The Stoic sage is free of the pathē, the irrational passions such as fear, grief and lust, which the Stoics thought were mistaken judgements. Apatheia is not indifference: the wise still have 'good feelings' like joy. The English 'apathy' has lost this sense.","The Stoics","Epictetus",("Stoicism",SEP+"stoicism/"),lang="grc")
card("t-epoche",2,"greek","ἐποχή","epochḗ","eh-po-KHAY","suspension, holding back","The Pyrrhonian Sceptics answered every claim with an equally strong counter-claim and so 'held back' from assent. Husserl revived the word in the 20th century for bracketing the question of whether the world exists, in order to describe experience exactly.","Pyrrho","Pyrrho",("Ancient scepticism",SEP+"skepticism-ancient/"),lang="grc")
card("t-oikeiosis",2,"greek","οἰκείωσις","oikeíōsis","oy-KAY-oh-sis","appropriation, making one's own","Stoic ethics starts from oikeiōsis: every animal from birth treats itself as its own and cares for its survival. In humans this concern widens, like circles round the self, to family, city and finally all humanity. It grounds Stoic cosmopolitanism.","The Stoics","Epictetus",("Oikeiôsis",WP+"Oikei%C3%B4sis"),lang="grc")
card("t-zoon-politikon",2,"greek","ζῷον πολιτικόν","zōion politikón","ZOH-on poh-lee-tee-KON","political animal","Aristotle defines the human being as an animal made by nature for life in a polis. Anyone who can live without a city is either a beast or a god. Speech (logos), which lets us share ideas of the just and unjust, is what makes us political.","Aristotle","Aristotle",("Aristotle's political theory",SEP+"aristotle-politics/"),lang="grc")
# III · Latin & Scholastic
card("t-tabula-rasa",3,"latin","tabula rasa","","TAH-boo-lah RAH-sah","scraped tablet, blank slate","The idea that the mind begins with no content of its own goes back to Aristotle, who compared the intellect to a writing tablet with nothing written on it. Medieval Latin called it tabula rasa. Locke made it the emblem of empiricism: all ideas come from experience.","Aristotle · Locke","Locke",("Tabula rasa",WP+"Tabula_rasa"),lang="la")
card("t-a-priori",3,"latin","a priori","","ah pree-OR-ee","from what is earlier","A priori knowledge is known independently of experience, as in mathematics or logic; a posteriori knowledge depends on it. Scholastics used the terms for reasoning from causes to effects and back. Kant reshaped them and asked how synthetic a priori judgements are possible.","Kant","Kant",("A priori and a posteriori",SEP+"apriori/"),("In allen theoretischen Wissenschaften der Vernunft sind synthetische Urteile a priori als Prinzipien enthalten","In all theoretical sciences of reason synthetic judgements a priori are contained as principles.","Critique of Pure Reason","B Introduction §V","g6343.txt"),lang="la")
card("t-haecceitas",3,"latin","haecceitas","","haik-KAY-ee-tahs","'thisness'","Duns Scotus asked what makes an individual this one and not another. His answer was a haecceitas, a positive principle of individuality distinct from the shared nature. The term has been revived in modern metaphysics for primitive identity.","Duns Scotus","Duns Scotus",("Medieval individuation",SEP+"medieval-haecceity/"),lang="la")
card("t-quidditas",3,"latin","quidditas","","KWID-dih-tahs","'whatness', essence","Quidditas is what a thing is: the answer to 'what is it?' (quid est?). Latin scholastics coined it to render Arabic and Greek terms for essence. Aquinas uses it for the essence expressed by a definition, as distinct from a thing's existence (esse).","Aquinas","Aquinas",("Quiddity",WP+"Quiddity"),lang="la")
card("t-actus-purus",3,"latin","actus purus","","AHK-toos POO-roos","pure act","Aquinas, following Aristotle, describes God as pure actuality, with no unrealised potential. Since change is passing from potential to actual, God cannot change, lack anything, or be composed of parts. It is the Latin heir of Aristotle's unmoved mover.","Aquinas","Aquinas",("Actus purus",WP+"Actus_purus"),lang="la")
card("t-esse",3,"latin","esse","","ES-seh","to be; existence","For Aquinas a creature's essence (what it is) is distinct from its esse (that it is). Only in God are they identical: God is ipsum esse subsistens, subsisting being itself. The distinction, prepared by Avicenna, is central to Thomist metaphysics.","Aquinas","Aquinas",("Aquinas",SEP+"aquinas/"),lang="la")
# IV · Early Modern
card("t-cogito",4,"latin","cogito, ergo sum","","KOH-gih-toh, ER-goh SOOM","I think, therefore I am","Descartes doubts everything that can be doubted, even mathematics, and finds one thing he cannot: that he is thinking, and so exists. He first wrote it in French in the Discourse on Method; the Latin form appears in the Principles of Philosophy. It became the founding certainty of modern philosophy.","Descartes","Descartes",("Descartes' epistemology",SEP+"descartes-epistemology/"),("je pense, donc je suis","I think, therefore I am.","Discourse on Method","Part 4 (1637)","g13846.txt"),lang="la")
card("t-res-cogitans",4,"latin","res cogitans","","RAYS KOH-gih-tahns","thinking thing","Descartes divides reality into two substances: res cogitans, mind, whose essence is thought, and res extensa, matter, whose essence is extension in space. How the two interact in a human being became the mind–body problem.","Descartes","Descartes",("Mind–body dualism",SEP+"dualism/"),lang="la")
card("t-conatus",4,"latin","conatus","","koh-NAH-toos","striving, endeavour","Spinoza holds that each thing, as far as it can, strives to persevere in its being, and that this striving is its very essence. Desire, joy and sadness are all variations of it. Hobbes and Leibniz used the term too, but Spinoza's version is the best known.","Spinoza","Spinoza",("Spinoza's psychology",SEP+"spinoza-psychological/"),("Unaquæque res quantum in se est, in suo esse perseverare conatur.","Each thing, as far as it is in itself, strives to persevere in its being.","Ethics","III, prop. 6","spin3.html"),lang="la")
card("t-deus-sive-natura",4,"latin","Deus sive Natura","","DAY-oos SEE-weh nah-TOO-rah","God, or Nature","Spinoza identifies God with the one infinite substance of which everything is a mode. 'God or Nature' means they are two names for the same thing. The Latin Library text quoted below spells it 'seu', a synonym of 'sive'. Readers have called the view atheism, pantheism, or the most rigorous theology ever written.","Spinoza","Spinoza",("Spinoza",SEP+"spinoza/"),("Ratio igitur seu causa cur Deus seu Natura agit et cur existit una eademque est.","So the reason or cause why God, or Nature, acts and why it exists is one and the same.","Ethics","IV, preface","spin4.html"),lang="la")
card("t-monade",4,"french","monade","","moh-NAD","unit (from Greek monas)","Leibniz's monads are simple, partless substances, the ultimate constituents of reality. They have no windows: nothing enters or leaves them, and each mirrors the whole universe from its own point of view. Their harmony is pre-established by God.","Leibniz","Leibniz",("Leibniz",SEP+"leibniz/"),("La Monade, dont nous parlerons ici, n'est autre chose, qu'une substance simple, qui entre dans les composés; simple, c'est-à-dire sans parties.","The Monad we shall speak of here is nothing but a simple substance that enters into composites; simple, that is, without parts.","Monadology","§1","g17641.txt"),lang="fr")
# V · Kant
card("t-ding-an-sich",5,"german","Ding an sich","","DING ahn ZIKH","thing in itself","Kant argues that we know objects only as they appear to us, shaped by the forms of space and time and the categories of the understanding. What things are in themselves, apart from our experience, we can think but never know. Fichte and Hegel rejected the idea as incoherent.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's transcendental idealism",SEP+"kant-transcendental-idealism/"),("das Objekt in zweierlei Bedeutung nehmen lehrt, nämlich als Erscheinung, oder als Ding an sich selbst","[it] teaches us to take the object in two senses, namely as appearance or as thing in itself.","Critique of Pure Reason","B Preface, Bxxvii","g6343.txt"))
card("t-erscheinung",5,"german","Erscheinung","","er-SHY-noong","appearance","For Kant an Erscheinung is an object as it is given to us in experience: in space and time and ordered by the categories. Appearances are not illusions; they are the only objects of possible knowledge. They contrast with things in themselves.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's transcendental idealism",SEP+"kant-transcendental-idealism/"))
card("t-anschauung",5,"german","Anschauung","","AHN-show-oong","intuition, looking-at","An Anschauung is a direct, singular representation of an object, given through the senses. Kant insists that knowledge needs both intuitions and concepts: the senses give, the understanding thinks. Space and time are the pure forms of human intuition.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's theory of judgment",SEP+"kant-judgment/"),("Gedanken ohne Inhalt sind leer, Anschauungen ohne Begriffe sind blind.","Thoughts without content are empty, intuitions without concepts are blind.","Critique of Pure Reason","A51/B75","g6343.txt"))
card("t-apperzeption",5,"german","transzendentale Apperzeption","","trans-tsen-den-TAH-leh ah-per-tsep-TSYOHN","transcendental apperception","The unity of self-consciousness that makes experience possible. Every representation I have must be able to be accompanied by 'I think', or it would be nothing to me. Kant calls this the highest point of all use of the understanding.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's view of the mind",SEP+"kant-mind/"),("Das: Ich denke, muß alle meine Vorstellungen begleiten können","The 'I think' must be able to accompany all my representations.","Critique of Pure Reason","§16, B131","g6343.txt"))
card("t-kategorischer-imperativ",5,"german","kategorischer Imperativ","","kah-teh-GOR-ish-er im-peh-rah-TEEF","categorical imperative","A moral command that binds unconditionally, whatever you want, unlike 'if you want X, do Y'. Kant's first formula: act only on a maxim you could will to become a universal law. Another: treat humanity never merely as a means but always also as an end.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's moral philosophy",SEP+"kant-moral/"))
card("t-aufklaerung",5,"german","Aufklärung","","OWF-klair-oong","clearing up; enlightenment","Kant's 1784 essay defines enlightenment as leaving behind a self-incurred immaturity: the inability to use one's own understanding without another's guidance. Its motto is 'Sapere aude!', dare to know. The word also names the whole 18th-century movement.","Kant","Kant",("What is Enlightenment?",WP+"Answering_the_Question:_What_Is_Enlightenment%3F"),("Aufklärung ist der Ausgang des Menschen aus seiner selbst verschuldeten Unmündigkeit.","Enlightenment is man's emergence from his self-incurred immaturity.","Was ist Aufklärung?","Berlinische Monatsschrift, 1784","aufkl.txt"))
card("t-erhabene",5,"german","das Erhabene","","dahs er-HAH-beh-neh","the sublime","In the Critique of Judgement Kant separates the beautiful, which pleases by harmony, from the sublime: the overwhelming feeling before a raging storm at sea or towering mountains. We feel small, but then discover in ourselves a reason greater than any nature.","Kant","Kant",("Kant's aesthetics",SEP+"kant-aesthetics/"))
# VII · Nietzsche & Schopenhauer
card("t-ubermensch",7,"german","Übermensch","","OO-ber-mensh","over-human, overman","Zarathustra announces the Übermensch as the goal for humanity after the death of God: a being who creates values instead of receiving them. Humanity is a rope stretched between animal and overman. The Nazis later misused the word; Nietzsche despised nationalism and anti-Semitism.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Übermensch",WP+"%C3%9Cbermensch"),("Ich lehre euch den Übermenschen. Der Mensch ist Etwas, das überwunden werden soll.","I teach you the overman. Man is something that shall be overcome.","Also sprach Zarathustra","Prologue §3","zara.txt"))
card("t-wille-zur-macht",7,"german","Wille zur Macht","","VIL-leh tsoor MAKHT","will to power","Nietzsche's name for the basic drive of all life: not merely to survive but to grow, expand and master. Self-preservation is only one of its consequences. He applied it to biology, psychology and morality alike, and saw philosophy itself as its most spiritual form.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Nietzsche",SEP+"nietzsche/"),("Leben selbst ist Wille zur Macht","Life itself is will to power.","Jenseits von Gut und Böse","§13","g7204.txt"))
card("t-ewige-wiederkunft",7,"german","ewige Wiederkunft","","AY-vih-geh VEE-der-koonft","eternal return","Suppose this life, every detail of it, were to recur again and again without end. Nietzsche presents this thought as a test: could you will it? Only someone who wholly affirms life could say yes. Zarathustra is called 'the teacher of the eternal return'.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Eternal return",WP+"Eternal_return"),("siehe, du bist der Lehrer der ewigen Wiederkunft","Behold, you are the teacher of the eternal return.","Also sprach Zarathustra","III, 'The Convalescent'","zara.txt"))
card("t-amor-fati",7,"latin","amor fati","","AH-mor FAH-tee","love of fate","Nietzsche's formula for human greatness: to want nothing to be different, forward or backward, for all eternity. Not only to bear what is necessary but to love it. The phrase is Latin, but the idea is his own.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Amor fati",WP+"Amor_fati"),("Meine Formel für die Grösse am Menschen ist amor fati: dass man Nichts anders haben will, vorwärts nicht, rückwärts nicht, in alle Ewigkeit nicht.","My formula for greatness in a human being is amor fati: that one wants nothing to be different, not forward, not backward, not in all eternity.","Ecce homo","'Why I Am So Clever' §10","g7202.txt"),lang="la")
card("t-ressentiment",7,"french","ressentiment","","ruh-sahn-tee-MAHN","resentment","Nietzsche used the French word for the festering, impotent hatred of the weak toward the strong. Unable to act, they take revenge in imagination by calling the strong 'evil' and themselves 'good'. He traced Christian morality to this 'slave revolt in morals'.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Nietzsche's moral philosophy",SEP+"nietzsche-moral-political/"),("Erst das Christenthum, mit seinem Ressentiment gegen das Leben auf dem Grunde, hat aus der Geschlechtlichkeit etwas Unreines gemacht","Only Christianity, with its ressentiment against life at bottom, made sexuality something impure.","Götzen-Dämmerung","'What I Owe the Ancients' §4","g7203.txt"),lang="fr")
card("t-apollinisch",7,"german","apollinisch / dionysisch","","ah-poh-LEE-nish / dee-oh-NOO-zish","Apollonian / Dionysian","In The Birth of Tragedy Nietzsche explains Greek tragedy as a union of two drives. The Apollonian is the drive to form, measure and beautiful illusion, like a dream; the Dionysian is ecstatic, boundary-dissolving intoxication. Tragedy lets us glimpse the terrible truth of existence and still affirm it.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("Apollonian and Dionysian",WP+"Apollonian_and_Dionysian"))
card("t-gott-ist-tot",7,"german","Gott ist tot","","GOT ist TOHT","God is dead","Nietzsche's diagnosis of modern Europe: belief in the Christian God has become unbelievable, and with it the foundation of Western values has collapsed, even if people have not yet noticed. The danger is nihilism; the task is a revaluation of all values.","Nietzsche","Nietzsche",("God is dead",WP+"God_is_dead"),("Gott ist todt; an seinem Mitleiden mit den Menschen ist Gott gestorben.","God is dead; God has died of his pity for man.","Also sprach Zarathustra","II, 'On the Pitying'","zara.txt"))
# IX · Heidegger
card("t-dasein",9,"german","Dasein","","DAH-zine","being-there, existence","Heidegger's word for the kind of being that we ourselves are: the being for whom its own being is an issue. He avoids 'consciousness' or 'subject' because Dasein is always already in a world, among things and others. In ordinary German it just means 'existence'.","Heidegger","Heidegger",("Heidegger",SEP+"heidegger/"))
card("t-in-der-welt-sein",9,"german","In-der-Welt-sein","","in-dair-VELT-zine","being-in-the-world","Dasein is not a mind looking out at a world of objects; it is from the start absorbed in a world of tasks and tools. The hyphens mark a single unified phenomenon. A hammer is first encountered in use, ready-to-hand, not as a thing with properties.","Heidegger","Heidegger",("Being and Time",WP+"Being_and_Time"))
card("t-sein-zum-tode",9,"german","Sein zum Tode","","ZINE tsoom TOH-deh","being-toward-death","Death is Dasein's ownmost possibility: no one can die my death for me. By facing it in anxiety rather than fleeing into 'they say everyone dies', Dasein can live authentically, owning its finite life.","Heidegger","Heidegger",("Heidegger",IEP+"heidegge/"))
# XI · Wittgenstein & analytic
card("t-sprachspiel",11,"analytic","Sprachspiel","","SHPRAKH-shpeel","language-game","Late Wittgenstein compares using words to playing games: there is no single essence of language, only many activities bound up with forms of life, such as giving orders, telling jokes, praying. The meaning of a word is its use in the language.","Wittgenstein","Wittgenstein",("Wittgenstein",SEP+"wittgenstein/"))
card("t-familienahnlichkeit",11,"analytic","Familienähnlichkeit","","fah-MEEL-yen-AYN-likh-kite","family resemblance","What do all games have in common? Nothing, says Wittgenstein: only overlapping similarities, like the resemblances among members of a family. Many concepts work this way, so asking for a strict definition can be a mistake.","Wittgenstein","Wittgenstein",("Family resemblance",WP+"Family_resemblance"))
